    <fr:title text="More Thoughts on Sequential Ambiguity">More Thoughts on Sequential Ambiguity</fr:title>
    <fr:taxon>Daily</fr:taxon>
  </fr:frontmatter>
  <fr:mainmatter>
    <fr:p>Previously Max suggested the internal definition of sequential ambiguity to be the following:</fr:p>
    <fr:tree show-metadata="false">
      <fr:frontmatter>
        <fr:authors>
          <fr:author>
            <fr:link href="maxsnew.xml" title="Max S. New" addr="maxsnew" type="local">Max S. New</fr:link>
          </fr:author>
          <fr:author>
            <fr:link href="stevenschaefer.xml" title="Steven Schaefer" addr="stevenschaefer" type="local">Steven Schaefer</fr:link>
          </fr:author>
        </fr:authors>
        <fr:date>
          <fr:year>2025</fr:year>
          <fr:month>1</fr:month>
          <fr:day>24</fr:day>
        </fr:date>
        <fr:anchor>527</fr:anchor>
        <fr:addr type="user">sss-0016</fr:addr>
        <fr:route>sss-0016.xml</fr:route>
        <fr:title text="Sequential Ambiguity Helper">Sequential Ambiguity Helper</fr:title>
        <fr:taxon>Daily</fr:taxon>
      </fr:frontmatter>
      <fr:mainmatter>
        <fr:p>Max suggested that an effective way to represent <fr:link href="sss-0017.xml" title="Sequential Unambiguity" addr="sss-0017" type="local">sequential unamiguity</fr:link> in Dependent Lambek Calculus to alleviate my issues proving <fr:link href="sss-000V.xml" title="FollowLast Soundness" addr="sss-000V" type="local">followlast soundness</fr:link>.</fr:p>
        <fr:p>An attempt would be that the following map has an inverse
  <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[g \otimes  {\mathopen {}\left ({\mathopen {}\left (h \otimes  k\right )\mathclose {}} \And  {\mathopen {}\left (h \otimes  l\right )\mathclose {}}\right )\mathclose {}} \vdash  {\mathopen {}\left (g \otimes  h \otimes  k\right )\mathclose {}} \And  (g \otimes  h \otimes  l)]]></fr:tex></fr:p>
      </fr:mainmatter>
    </fr:tree>
    <fr:p>After more thought, I think this is close but not exactly right. The attempt at the definition would presuppose that <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[g]]></fr:tex> is unambiguous. However the following definition would allow for ambiguity in <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[g]]></fr:tex>, and has been proven in Agda by me.</fr:p>
    <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[(g \And  g) \otimes  {\mathopen {}\left ({\mathopen {}\left (h \otimes  k\right )\mathclose {}} \And  {\mathopen {}\left (h \otimes  l\right )\mathclose {}}\right )\mathclose {}} \cong  {\mathopen {}\left (g \otimes  h \otimes  k\right )\mathclose {}} \And  (g \otimes  h \otimes  l)]]></fr:tex>
    <fr:p>Rather than requiring an inverse that mystically picks out a <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[g]]></fr:tex>-parse, the above isomorphism maintains both <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[g]]></fr:tex> parses but faithfully reflects that the splitting point between <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[g]]></fr:tex> and <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[h]]></fr:tex> is uniquely determined.</fr:p>
  </fr:mainmatter>
